**Module layout**

```
mylib-parent (pom)
mylib-spring-boot-autoconfigure
  src/main/java/com/example/mylib/
    MyLibAutoConfiguration.java
    MyLibProperties.java
  src/main/resources/META-INF/spring/
    org.springframework.boot.autoconfigure.AutoConfiguration.imports
mylib-spring-boot-starter
  pom.xml (depends on mylib-core + mylib-spring-boot-autoconfigure)
```

**Auto-configuration class**

```java
@AutoConfiguration
@ConditionalOnClass(MyCoreLibrary.class)
@EnableConfigurationProperties(MyLibProperties.class)
public class MyLibAutoConfiguration {

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnMissingBean
    public MyService myService(MyLibProperties props) {
        return new MyService(props.getEndpoint(), props.getTimeout());
    }

    @Bean
    @ConditionalOnProperty(value = "mylib.metrics.enabled",
                           havingValue = "true", matchIfMissing = true)
    public MetricsExporter metricsExporter(MeterRegistry registry) {
        return new MetricsExporter(registry);
    }
}
```

**Properties class (record-based, Spring Boot 3.x)**

```java
@ConfigurationProperties("mylib")
public record MyLibProperties(
    String endpoint,
    Duration timeout
) {
    public MyLibProperties {
        endpoint = endpoint != null ? endpoint : "https://default.example.com";
        timeout = timeout != null ? timeout : Duration.ofSeconds(5);
    }
}
```

**`AutoConfiguration.imports` content**

```
com.example.mylib.MyLibAutoConfiguration
```

**Starter POM (empty, only dependencies)**

```xml
<dependencies>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>mylib-core</artifactId>
    </dependency>
    <dependency>
        <groupId>com.example</groupId>
        <artifactId>mylib-spring-boot-autoconfigure</artifactId>
    </dependency>
</dependencies>
```

**Consumer usage**

```yaml
mylib:
  endpoint: https://prod.example.com
  timeout: 3s
```

- `@ConditionalOnMissingBean` lets the consumer override the default `MyService` by declaring their own `@Bean`
- The two-module split means you can release `mylib-core` independently of the Spring integration layer

**Why `ApplicationContextRunner`**
- Spins up a minimal context with just your auto-config — no full `@SpringBootTest` overhead
- Tests run in milliseconds
- Lets you assert on bean presence, properties, and exclusion behavior

**Test class**

```java
class MyLibAutoConfigurationTest {

    private final ApplicationContextRunner contextRunner =
        new ApplicationContextRunner()
            .withConfiguration(AutoConfigurations.of(MyLibAutoConfiguration.class));

    @Test
    void registersDefaultService() {
        contextRunner.run(ctx -> {
            assertThat(ctx).hasSingleBean(MyService.class);
            assertThat(ctx.getBean(MyService.class).getEndpoint())
                .isEqualTo("https://default.example.com");
        });
    }

    @Test
    void userOverrideWins() {
        contextRunner
            .withBean(MyService.class, () -> new MyService("override", Duration.ZERO))
            .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx.getBean(MyService.class).getEndpoint())
                .isEqualTo("override"));
    }

    @Test
    void appliesProperties() {
        contextRunner
            .withPropertyValues("mylib.endpoint=http://test", "mylib.timeout=10s")
            .run(ctx -> assertThat(ctx.getBean(MyService.class).getEndpoint())
                .isEqualTo("http://test"));
    }
}
```

**IDE completion via the configuration processor**

```xml
<dependency>
    <groupId>org.springframework.boot</groupId>
    <artifactId>spring-boot-configuration-processor</artifactId>
    <optional>true</optional>
</dependency>
```

- Generates `META-INF/spring-configuration-metadata.json` at compile time
- IntelliJ and VS Code plugins use it to auto-complete `mylib.*` keys with type hints and Javadoc
- Without the processor, custom properties show as 'unknown property' warnings
